<# Markdown twin of the cover letter — the third rendering of one letter.json, beside
   coverletter.tex.njk (the PDF) and coverletter.txt.njk (paste-into-a-form plain text).
   Same paragraphs, same order. If the three ever say different things, the .json is not the
   single source and something has gone wrong.

   THIS ONE EXISTS TO BE EDITED BY SOMEONE ELSE. It gets pasted into a Google Doc, marked up by
   a supervisor or a reviewer, and exported as a PDF from there. Three consequences shape it:

   1. NO PROVENANCE IN THE BODY. Citation tokens live only in the Review notes table at the
      bottom, so what a reviewer reads is a letter, not a letter interleaved with machinery.
      Rendered with banner: false for the same reason — the standard provenance header is a
      LaTeX `%` comment and would paste in as visible garbage.

   2. THE DERIVED WARNING IS A BLOCKQUOTE, not an HTML comment. An HTML comment survives a
      Google Docs paste as visible text and reads as a bug; a blockquote reads as a note and is
      one keystroke to delete. It has to be visible somewhere, because this file IS derived and
      edits made here are destroyed by the next `npm run tailor`.

   3. `untex` unwinds the LaTeX that hand-authored data legitimately contains ($\mu$, \&, ---),
      exactly as the .txt twin does. Markdown is not LaTeX.

   `criterion` is optional per paragraph. When present it names what the paragraph is there to
   answer (a job requirement, a promotion criterion), and appears only in the Review notes —
   which is what lets a reviewer check the letter against the thing it must satisfy rather than
   just reading it for tone. verify-application.js ignores the field. #>
